# Digests of the wasm-pack artifacts this crate embeds with include_bytes!/include_str!.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# sdks/verifier-js/pkg/ is gitignored: it is a wasm-pack build product, not a
# source. So the bytes this crate compiles in are invisible to git, which means
# invisible to any read-set built from tracked files -- nucleus-action-key's
# closure refuses to key this crate for exactly that reason. The artifact could
# change with nothing in the tree noticing, and this crate is the relying-party
# verifier.
#
# Tracking the bytes themselves was the alternative and was rejected: 1.29 MB of
# unmergeable binary churning on every rebuild, in a project whose own records
# cite a 15.8-minute checkout caused by 3.3 GiB of committed build artifacts.
#
# A digest is ~70 bytes and moves whenever the artifact moves. build.rs refuses
# to compile on a mismatch, which is what makes the pin a guarantee rather than
# a comment: a tracked digest nothing verifies is a lie that reads like a
# promise.
#
# REPRODUCIBLE ON ONE MACHINE, NOT ACROSS PLATFORMS. Measured 2026-09-14: two
# clean wasm-pack 0.13.1 builds of the same source on one host produced
# byte-identical output, so a pin is meaningful. The SAME source and the SAME
# wasm-pack on a different platform did not: macOS aarch64 gave
# 839c9e74299669cb..., Linux x86_64 in CI gave 8b4ca42f7cc88a74...
#
# Both digests below moved on 2026-09-15 because the SOURCE changed, not the
# platform: nucleus-recompute depends on nucleus-receipt, which gained 7 lines,
# and the wasm embeds it. The .js shim did NOT move (e12ff811... on both
# platforms, before and after), which is a second measurement of the claim
# above and the reason one line still suffices for it. The stale digests were
# REPLACED, not appended: a pin that still accepts a wasm built from superseded
# source accepts exactly the drift it exists to catch.
#
# So these digests track ONE canonical builder, and that builder is CI, because
# CI produces what ships. A local rebuild on another platform will differ and
# the build will refuse -- correctly, since the bytes really are not the ones
# that were reviewed. What this pin catches is drift on the canonical builder;
# it is not a claim of cross-platform reproducibility, and the difference is
# worth knowing rather than papering over.
#
# Regenerate from a CI run after an intentional SDK change; build.rs reports
# every mismatching artifact at once so one run supplies them all:
#   wasm-pack build sdks/verifier-js --target web --release
#   shasum -a 256 sdks/verifier-js/pkg/nucleus_verifier_wasm_bg.wasm \
#     sdks/verifier-js/pkg/nucleus_verifier_wasm.js
#
# Format: <sha256>  <repo-relative path>, as shasum -a 256 emits. A path may be
# listed more than once; the build accepts any digest listed for it, because
# wasm-pack output is not reproducible across platforms and a single-digest pin
# would break whichever platform did not mint it. An artifact matching NO listed
# digest still fails, which is the property worth keeping.
#
# Notably only the .wasm differs by platform: the .js shim came out identical on
# both, so one line suffices for it.
#
# 2026-09-15 (#2509): the .wasm moved again. Sealing `CreditEvent` changed
# `nucleus-creditworthiness`, which `sdks/verifier-js` depends on, so the
# compiled bytes changed with it; the .js shim did NOT move, which is the same
# "only the .wasm differs" observation holding for a source change rather than a
# platform change.
#
# Both prior .wasm digests are REPLACED by the single CI one, per the rule
# above: they are the pre-seal artifact, and a pin that still accepted them
# would accept a verifier compiled from source WITHOUT the seal — the drift this
# pin exists to catch. That leaves one .wasm digest until another platform mints
# a build of this source worth listing.
#
# The digest below was taken from the CI run on this branch rather than
# generated locally, because CI is the canonical builder and a local build would
# differ by platform anyway (see above). It is therefore UNVERIFIED HERE by
# construction — what verifies it is build.rs recomputing the digest from the
# artifact CI just built: if this line were wrong, the build refuses. The pin is
# confirmed by the run that consumes it, not by the hand that wrote it.
#
# 2026-09-15 (#2510): and again, for the same reason one layer out. The
# countersigned-clearing envelope changed `nucleus-recompute`, which
# `sdks/verifier-js` also compiles in, so the bytes moved past #2509's
# `20b10eb3…`. That digest is REPLACED rather than kept alongside, on the rule
# above: it is the pre-countersignature artifact, and accepting it would accept a
# verifier built from source without the two-party check. The .js shim did not
# move for this change either — two independent source changes now, and it has
# held both times.
#
# Same provenance caveat as #2509's: taken from the CI run on this branch, so
# unverified at the point of writing and verified by the run that consumes it.

# 2026-09-18 (#2924): the .wasm moved again, and again because the SOURCE did --
# verifying checkpoints, envelope metadata and the receipt version changed
# `nucleus-envelope` and `nucleus-receipt`, which `sdks/verifier-js` compiles in.
# `20b10eb3…`'s successor `9f7e477c…` is REPLACED rather than kept, on the rule
# above: it is the pre-verification artifact and accepting it would accept a
# verifier built from source that does not make these checks.
#
# Worth recording against the cross-platform caveat at the top: this digest was
# minted TWICE independently, by GitHub's x86_64 runner and by the gate lane's
# linux/arm64 microVM, and they agree byte for byte. That is the first time the
# two platforms have been observed to produce the same .wasm, so one line
# suffices here for a reason stronger than convention -- though it is one
# observation, not a claim that the difference has gone away.
# 2026-09-21 (#3002): the .wasm moved for a source change with NO behaviour
# change — `nucleus-recompute` turned its `use nucleus_econ_kernels::{…}` into a
# `pub use`, so a receipt-checking consumer can spell `IntegerBid` without
# depending on an economic crate. Nothing the verifier COMPUTES changed; the
# exported symbol set did, and the compiled bytes moved with it. Recorded
# because it is the first time this pin moved without the verifier's behaviour
# moving, and the honest reading of that is that the pin tracks the artifact,
# not the semantics — which is what makes it a drift check rather than a proof
# of equivalence.
#
# `54eaafbe…` is REPLACED, on the standing rule: it is the artifact built before
# the re-export, and a pin listing both would accept either, which is exactly
# the drift the file exists to notice. Same provenance caveat as every digest
# here — taken from the CI run on this branch, so unverified at the point of
# writing and verified by the run that consumes it.
# 2026-09-24: MERGED main INTO this branch, and this digest is a PLACEHOLDER that the next CI
# run must replace. Neither side of the conflict was right. main pinned `69a16e5d…`, built from
# main's source; this branch pinned `75fe617e…`, built from its own pre-merge source; and the
# merged tree is neither, because this branch changes ten of the crates `sdks/verifier-js`
# embeds. `75fe617e…` is kept only because a file with conflict markers does not parse -- it is
# this branch's own last honest claim, and it is now stale by construction.
#
# Listing BOTH digests would have made the conflict disappear and is exactly what the header
# above forbids: "a pin that still accepts a wasm built from superseded source accepts exactly
# the drift it exists to catch." A local rebuild is no better -- the digest differs by platform,
# so it would be a number this builder produced and CI never will.
#
# So build.rs is EXPECTED to refuse on the first run after this merge. That refusal is the pin
# working. Take the digest it reports and REPLACE the line below.
75fe617ee401703c98c1ecfe99f8774a95b4ade42bcf1c26cd316fb3215e4264  sdks/verifier-js/pkg/nucleus_verifier_wasm_bg.wasm
e12ff8119a5e13c332081d2bf96d64a7869e345b7ff3205c548f88cd27a1aae2  sdks/verifier-js/pkg/nucleus_verifier_wasm.js
